// 6. Lack of Comments or Documentation  
// Methods that lack adequate comments or documentation make it more difficult for other developers to understand the intent behind the code and maintain it.  
// The Maintainability Index often considers the ratio of comments to code,  
// and methods with few or no comments typically have lower MI scores.  
// While comments are not a direct substitute for clean, self-explanatory code,  
// a lack of comments can signal that the code may be complex or difficult to comprehend.  

// 7. Use of Global State or Static Variables  
// Methods that rely heavily on global variables or static state tend to be harder to maintain,  
// as they introduce tight coupling between different parts of the code.  
// Changes in global state can have far-reaching, unintended effects across the system, making the method more error-prone.  
// These types of methods often have lower MI scores,  
// as they are difficult to test and maintain due to their dependencies on mutable, global state.  

// 8. Frequent Changes or Modifications  
// If a method is frequently changed or modified, it might indicate that the method is poorly designed or too complex,  
// leading to its constant need for adjustments.  
// Methods that require frequent changes are often harder to maintain because developers may not fully understand their design or underlying issues.  
// These methods can lower the MI score due to their lack of stability and the increased cost of maintaining them over time.  

// How the Maintainability Index Helps Identify Not Maintainable Methods  
// The Maintainability Index is calculated using a formula that considers factors like cyclomatic complexity, lines of code, and Halstead complexity measures.  
