// 5. Increase Documentation  
// Add meaningful comments and documentation where necessary,  
// especially for complex or non-obvious logic.  
// This helps other developers (or your future self) understand the code more easily.  

// 6. Reduce Dependencies on Global State  
// Minimize the use of global variables or static state within methods.  
// This makes the code more modular, easier to test, and less prone to side effects.  

// In conclusion, methods with a low Maintainability Index score are considered difficult to maintain  
// due to factors like excessive complexity, size, or poor readability.  
// By addressing these issues through refactoring, breaking down methods, reducing duplication,  
// and following good design practices, developers can improve the maintainability of their code  
// and ultimately raise the MI score for their methods.  

// </Description>]]></Query>
        <Query Active="True" Locked="True" DisplayList="True" DisplayStat="False" DisplaySelectionView="False" IsCriticalRule="False"><![CDATA[//<Name>Complex methods</Name>
from m in Methods where m.CyclomaticComplexity>20 && m.CyclomaticComplexity<40
select new {m,m.CyclomaticComplexity}
//<Description>
// Cyclomatic Complexity Categories  

// 1. 1-10: Low Complexity (Simple Methods)  
// Description: Methods in this range are straightforward, with minimal branching or looping.  
// They are easy to test, debug, and maintain.  
// Examples:  
// - Simple calculations or data manipulations.  
// - Single if or switch statement.  
// - Basic loops with minimal conditions.  
// Recommended Action:  
// - No immediate concerns.  
// - Ensure methods remain concise and single-responsibility.  

// 2. 11-20: Moderate Complexity  
// Description: Methods start to show more decision-making paths, loops, and nested conditions.  
// While still manageable, these methods may begin to obscure their intent.  
// Examples:  
// - Methods with nested loops or if-else chains.  
// - Processing logic with multiple branching paths.  
// Recommended Action:  
// - Refactor if possible to reduce complexity.  
// - Consider splitting into smaller methods for better readability and testability.  

// 3. 21-30: High Complexity  
// Description: Methods in this range involve significant branching and are difficult to follow.  
// Testing becomes more challenging due to the increased number of code paths.  
// Examples:  
// - Large switch statements with many cases.  
// - Deeply nested loops or conditions.  
// - Methods with numerous logical decisions in sequence.  
// Recommended Action:  
// - Break down into smaller methods or delegate tasks to helper methods.  
// - Rethink method design and apply design patterns if necessary (e.g., strategy or state pattern).  

// 4. 31-50: Very High Complexity  
// Description: Methods are extremely intricate, involving numerous paths, loops, and conditions.  
// They are prone to bugs and are very difficult to test or maintain.  
// Examples:  
// - Large, monolithic methods with complex error-handling logic.  
// - Methods performing multiple unrelated tasks.  
// - Code with deeply nested try-catch blocks.  
// Recommended Action:  
// - Immediate refactoring is strongly recommended.  
// - Consider reorganizing functionality into smaller, modular methods.  
// - Avoid mixing too many responsibilities into one method.  

// 5. 50+: Extreme Complexity (Unmaintainable)  
// Description: Methods with this level of complexity are almost impossible to maintain.  
// They are a significant risk to project stability and quality.  
// Examples:  
// - Legacy code or "god methods" handling an entire process in one block.  
// - Excessive use of nested conditions, loops, and error-handling constructs.  
// - Code that lacks separation of concerns.  
// Recommended Action:  
// - Rewrite the method entirely.  
// - Redesign the logic using modern principles, such as SOLID and clean architecture.  
// - Eliminate unnecessary complexity by delegating functionality to other classes or methods.  

// General Guidelines for Managing Complexity  
// 1. Break Down Large Methods: Use helper methods to break down functionality into smaller, reusable components. Aim for each method to handle a single responsibility.  
// 2. Use Design Patterns: Apply appropriate design patterns to handle complex logic elegantly.  
// 3. Refactor Regularly: Avoid letting methods grow unchecked. Periodically review code for opportunities to simplify.  
// 4. Write Unit Tests: Test all possible paths through complex methods to identify bugs early.  
// 5. Document Logic: Add comments or documentation to explain non-obvious decision-making in the code.  

// By following these guidelines and categorizing methods based on cyclomatic complexity,  
// you can keep your codebase clean, testable, and maintainable.  

//Description]]></Query>
        <Query Active="True" Locked="True" DisplayList="True" DisplayStat="False" DisplaySelectionView="False" IsCriticalRule="False"><![CDATA[//<Name>Too complex methods</Name>
from m in Methods where m.CyclomaticComplexity>=40
select new {m,m.CyclomaticComplexity}
